course: physics
lesson: 8
title: Balance: the centre of gravity
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every object acts as if all its weight sat at one point, its centre of gravity, and it balances when that point is right above what holds it up.
explain: Balance a ruler on one finger and it stays only when your finger is under its middle; stick clay on one end and the balancing point moves towards the clay.
explain: Things tip over when their centre of gravity goes outside their base, so wide, low things are hard to knock over and tall, narrow ones topple.
explain: Your body balances all the time without you noticing: when you lean forward, your bottom goes back, and your eyes and inner ears tell your brain which way you are tilting.
fact: Every object acts as if all its weight sat at one point, called its centre of gravity.
fact: A ruler balances on your finger when your finger is right under its middle; a lump of clay on one end moves the balancing point towards the clay.
fact: Standing on one leg, you keep moving to keep your centre of gravity over your foot, and with your eyes shut it gets much harder.
fact: A wobbly toy stands back up because its heavy weight sits low in its round base, and ships carry heavy weight low down for the same reason.
fact: Something tips over only when its centre of gravity goes outside its base, so wide, low things are hard to tip.
fact: Racing cars are built low and wide so they do not tip over in fast corners.
fact: When you lean forward, your hips go back to keep your balance, so with your back against a wall you cannot touch your toes.
fact: A tightrope walker's long pole is slow to turn, which gives the walker time to correct wobbles.
fact: People lean away from a heavy bag to bring their centre of gravity back over their feet, and two bags, one in each hand, are easier than one twice as heavy.
fact: On a seesaw, a heavy person near the middle can balance a light person far from the middle.
fact: A tall thing tips over slowly, so a broom is easier to balance on your palm than a pencil.
fact: Tiny tubes of liquid in your inner ear tell your brain which way you are tilting, and after spinning the liquid keeps moving, which makes you dizzy.
exercise: Where is the balancing point of a ruler? | Right in its middle | At one of its ends | Wherever the numbers start | It has no balancing point
exercise: What is the centre of gravity of an object? | The point where all its weight seems to sit | The heaviest part, which is always on its outside edge | The middle of its top side, where you would hold it up | The spot on it where the pull of gravity is the strongest
exercise: You stick a lump of clay on one end of the ruler. Where does the balancing point move? | Towards the clay | Away from the clay | It stays in the middle | It disappears completely
exercise: Why is it harder to balance on one leg with your eyes shut? | Your eyes help you balance | Your leg gets weaker in the dark | Gravity is stronger in the dark | Your ears switch off when eyes close
exercise: Why does the wobbly toy always stand back up? | Its heavy weight is low in its round base | It is full of light air at the bottom | It has a spring inside its head | Gravity pulls its top upwards
exercise: Why do ships carry heavy weight low down? | So they roll back upright in a storm | So they go faster through the big waves at sea | So the sailors have more room to walk about on deck | So they float higher and stay drier in the water
exercise: Why does a tall stack of blocks topple but a wide pyramid does not? | The pyramid has a wide base and low weight | The pyramid blocks are stickier and hold on to each other | The tall stack is much lighter | The pyramid is pulled down harder
exercise: When does something tip over? | When its centre of gravity goes outside its base | Whenever it is taller than it is wide, even a tiny bit | When it is touched by anything | When it is heavier at the bottom
exercise: Why are racing cars so low and wide? | So they are hard to tip over | So they fit into small garages | So the wind lifts them up | So they are lighter to carry
exercise: Why can't you touch your toes with your back against a wall? | Your hips cannot go back to keep you balanced | The wall pulls you backwards | Your legs are too short to reach when a wall is right behind you | Your arms get shorter when you lean
exercise: Why do tightrope walkers hold a long pole? | It turns slowly, giving them time to fix wobbles | It makes them lighter on the rope | It helps them push off the ground | It is used to catch them if they fall
exercise: Why do people lean away from a heavy bag? | To bring their balance point back over their feet | To make the heavy bag feel lighter in their hand somehow | To stop the bag from swinging out into the passing cars | Because the bag pushes them over and they cannot stop it
exercise: Which is easier to carry? | Two bags, one in each hand | One bag twice as heavy in one hand | Both are just as easy | One bag on one finger
exercise: Where should a heavy child sit on a seesaw to balance a little one? | Nearer the middle | At the very end | On the same spot as the little one | It does not matter where
exercise: Why is a long broom easier to balance on your palm than a pencil? | A tall thing tips over slowly | A broom is lighter than a pencil | Pencils are pulled more by gravity | Brooms have a sticky handle
exercise: Why do you feel dizzy after spinning? | The liquid in your inner ear keeps moving | Your brain keeps spinning round and round inside your head | Your blood all goes to your feet | Your eyes keep turning round
test: My cousin says you balance only with your feet. right? | No, your eyes and inner ears help a lot | Yes, feet do all the balancing | No, you balance with your arms only | Yes, that is why shoes have soles
test: Try it: back against the wall, touch your toes. What happens? | You tip forward or give up | You touch them easily | You fall backwards through the wall | You float up a little
test: A dad and a toddler want to balance on a seesaw. What works? | The dad sits very close to the middle | The dad sits at the very end | The toddler sits in the middle and the dad sits at the end | It cannot be done at all
test: In a film a giant tall thin tower stands on one tiny point. What would really happen? | It would topple as soon as it leaned | It would stand there for ever without moving | It would float slowly upwards into the clouds | It would sink straight down into the ground
test: Why do you hold your arms out when walking along a narrow wall? | Like a pole, they help you correct wobbles | They make you lighter | They catch the wind like sails and lift you up a little | They pull you to the ground
test: What would happen to a ship with all its heavy cargo stacked high on deck? | It would roll over more easily in a storm | It would be safer in a storm | It would float much higher | It would go faster
test: oi stop spinning, ur gonna fall over. why tho | The liquid in your ears keeps spinning and fools your brain | Spinning makes your legs heavier | Spinning pulls your blood up | Spinning makes the floor tilt
test: Your bag of shopping is heavy on the left. Which way do you lean? | To the right | To the left | Forwards | You do not need to lean
test: Why is balancing a pencil on your finger almost impossible? | It tips over too fast to correct | It is far too heavy | Pencils have no centre of gravity | It is too long and thin to see
test: A toy has a heavy bottom and is pushed over. What happens? | It rolls back upright | It stays lying down | It spins on its side | It falls through the table
test: Which stands most safely on a bus? | Feet wide apart, knees a bit bent | Feet together on tiptoe | One foot, arms up | Standing tall with feet close
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the condition under which a body will be overturned? | Its centre of gravity passes beyond the limits of its base | Its height exceeds its width by any amount | Its weight is concentrated at its lowest part | It is touched by any external body
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a tightrope performer carries an elongated pole. | The pole resists rapid rotation and so affords time for correction | The pole diminishes the weight of the performer | The pole is employed to propel the performer forwards | The pole conducts the performer's weight to the ground
exam: It is given that two persons of unequal weight are to balance upon a seesaw. Determine the requisite disposition of the heavier person. | Nearer to the pivot than the lighter person | Further from the pivot than the lighter person | At an equal distance from the pivot | Directly above the pivot itself
exam: Ascertain the organ chiefly responsible for informing the brain of the inclination of the body. | The inner ear | The stomach | The skin of the feet | The lungs
